course: biology
lesson: 259
title: The microscopic world: tardigrades, mites, plankton and other tiny creatures
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Scoop a clump of wet moss from a wall, squeeze a drop onto a slide, and you may meet a tardigrade, or water bear: a plump little animal less than a millimetre long, lumbering about on eight stubby legs.
explain: When its moss dries out, a tardigrade curls into a dry little barrel called a «tun» and almost stops living; add water, even years later, and it can wake up and walk off again.
explain: Tiny creatures are all around us: dust mites in our beds, eyelash mites at the roots of our lashes, water fleas in ponds, and in the sea, drifting plankton that feed fish and even the giant blue whale.
explain: Cartoons sometimes show plankton as one small green character with one eye, but real plankton are millions of different drifting living things, from glassy algae to baby crabs and fish eggs.
explain: Most life on Earth is too small to see with our eyes, and scientists still discover thousands of new microscopic kinds every year.
fact: Tardigrades, or water bears, are tiny eight-legged animals less than a millimetre long that often live in moss; they were first described by Johann Goeze in 1773, who called them little water bears.
fact: A dried tardigrade curls into a «tun» and nearly stops all life processes, and in 2007 dried tardigrades survived 10 days in open space on a satellite.
fact: Dried tardigrades survive great cold, high pressure and radiation, but they are not immortal, and active tardigrades are quite fragile.
fact: Dust mites are tiny relatives of spiders, with eight legs, that live in beds and carpets and eat flakes of dead skin; their droppings can cause allergies and asthma, and washing bedding hot and airing it keeps them down.
fact: Most adults have tiny Demodex mites living in the roots of their eyelashes and in face pores, eating skin oils, usually without any harm; it was long said they have no bottom, but a 2022 study found they do.
fact: Plankton are tiny living things drifting in the sea, including algae, baby crabs, fish eggs and krill, and they are the base of the sea's food web.
fact: The blue whale, the biggest animal ever, eats tiny krill, up to several tonnes a day.
fact: Water fleas are tiny see-through crustaceans that jump through water with their antennae, and under a microscope you can watch their hearts beating.
fact: Rotifers are tiny animals with a crown of beating hairs that looks like spinning wheels, and some were reported to revive after about 24,000 years frozen in Siberian ice, because frozen they stop nearly all life processes, like a dried tardigrade.
fact: Diatoms are single-celled algae with glass shells in beautiful patterns, and they make a big share of the oxygen we breathe.
fact: The smallest insects are fairy wasps, sometimes called fairy flies; some are only about a seventh of a millimetre long, smaller than some single-celled creatures.
fact: «Sea monkeys» are brine shrimps whose dried eggs can wait for years and hatch in salt water, so when their salty lake dries up, the eggs wait until the water comes back; in the wild they live in very salty lakes where fish cannot live.
fact: A slime mould is a giant single cell with many nuclei; it can find the shortest way through a maze to food, and in a Japanese test it grew paths very like the Tokyo railway map.
fact: One drop of seawater can hold millions of viruses and bacteria and many plankton.
exercise: How many legs does a tardigrade have? | Eight | Twelve | Twenty | Sixteen
exercise: Where can you often find tardigrades? | In wet moss | In hot sand dunes | Inside apples | High up in clouds
exercise: What does a tardigrade do when its home dries out? | Curls into a dry «tun» and almost stops living | Digs a deep burrow down in the soil and sleeps there until it rains | Flies away to find water | Lays eggs and dies at once
exercise: What did dried tardigrades survive in 2007? | Ten days in open space on a satellite | A whole year at the bottom of a hot, bubbling volcano | A trip to the Moon and back | A million years in ice
exercise: Which statement about tardigrades is true? | Dried ones are very tough, but they are not immortal | They can never be killed by anything, not even by fire | They live for ever when they are active | They are the biggest animals living in moss
exercise: What do dust mites eat? | Flakes of dead skin | Blood from sleeping people | Wool fluff from old blankets | Crumbs of bread from the bed
exercise: Which animals are dust mites related to? | Spiders | Wood ants | Earthworms | Cat fleas
exercise: Where do Demodex mites live? | In the roots of eyelashes and in face pores | Inside the stomach, among the food | In the hair of pet cats and dogs only, never on people | In the middle of bones
exercise: What did a 2022 study find about eyelash mites? | They do have a bottom, though people said they did not | They live for a hundred years, longer than most people | They can jump from person to person like fleas | They glow in the dark
exercise: What does the blue whale mainly eat? | Tiny krill | Great white sharks | Green seaweed | Seals on the ice
exercise: Why is it fun to look at a water flea under a microscope? | Its body is see-through, so you can watch its heart beat | It sings a tiny song when it is magnified | It changes colour every second, from red to blue to green and back | It waves at you with its legs
exercise: What does a rotifer's crown of hairs look like? | Spinning wheels | A tiny hat | A pair of wings | A long tail
exercise: What are diatom shells made of? | Glass | Hard bone | Old wood | Copper
exercise: What is the smallest kind of insect? | A fairy wasp | A house fly | A flea | An ant
exercise: What are «sea monkeys» really? | Brine shrimps | Baby monkeys | Tiny fish | Sea snails
exercise: What is a slime mould? | A giant single cell with many nuclei | A kind of tiny yellow slug that lives in woods | A plant with yellow flowers | A pile of rotten egg
exercise: What did a slime mould do in the test in Japan? | Grew paths very like the Tokyo railway map | Ate its way through a whole toy train | Built a tiny model of Tokyo, with all of its streets | Found its way out of a fridge
test: In SpongeBob, Plankton is a tiny green character with one eye who plots all day. What are real plankton? | Many different tiny drifting living things, such as algae, krill and baby crabs | One small kind of one-eyed animal that swims in big shoals everywhere in the sea | Tiny fish that live in pineapples | Green rocks on the sea floor
test: Count them: under the microscope you see 3 tardigrades and 2 dust mites. How many legs in all? | 40 | 30 | 20 | 34
test: «tardigrades are immortal, they can't die, everyone knows» What is right? | No, dried ones are very tough, but active ones are fragile and they do die | Yes, nothing at all can ever kill them, not even fire, ice or empty space | Yes, but only in space | No, they die the moment they dry out
test: «my sis has bugs in her eyelashes?? gross, she's dirty» What is right? | Most adults have harmless eyelash mites; it is not about being dirty | Only dirty people who never wash their faces have them living there | They come from not brushing teeth | Nobody has them except animals
test: How can the biggest animal ever live on some of the smallest? | It gulps huge amounts of tiny krill, several tonnes a day | It eats only one krill a week | It eats the krill's eggs, which are huge, as big as footballs | It does not eat at all, it just drinks water
test: What would happen to the sea if all the plankton vanished? | The food web would collapse, from fish to whales | Fish would eat more seaweed and be fine | The sea would get more crowded | Whales would start eating land animals
test: Why would eggs that survive drying be useful in a salty lake? | When the lake dries up, the eggs wait and hatch when water returns | Dry eggs float better in salt | Birds cannot find the dry eggs, because they look just like grains of sand | Dry eggs are too salty for birds
test: «a slime mould solved a maze? it doesn't even have a brain lol» What is right? | Yes, with no brain at all it still found the shortest way to food | No, it had a tiny hidden brain inside it | No, the scientists drew the path on it themselves with a pen | Yes, because it is really a clever worm
test: Count them: 4 water fleas each have 2 big antennae for jumping. How many antennae is that? | 8 | 6 | 4 | 16
test: Mum shakes out the duvet and washes the sheets hot. Why does it help with dust mites? | It removes mites and their skin food and kills many of them | Mites hate the smell of soap only | It makes all the mites move down to the carpet for good, where they stay | It does nothing, mites live only in walls
test: How could rotifers survive about 24,000 years in ice? | Frozen, they stopped nearly all life processes until they thawed | They ate the ice slowly, a little bit at a time, to stay alive | They kept warm by swimming | They were born just before scientists found them
exam: Identify the principal means by which a tardigrade survives the desiccation of its habitat. | The contraction into a dry form termed a «tun», wherein its life processes very nearly cease | The excavation of a burrow beneath the moss | Migration to a more humid locality | The production of a waterproof shell of glass, within which it remains sealed until the rains return
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nourishment of the blue whale. | It subsists principally upon krill, consuming up to several tonnes thereof daily | It subsists principally upon large sharks | It consumes no food during its adult life | It subsists upon seaweed alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Demodex mites in respect of their human hosts? | They inhabit the roots of the eyelashes of most adults and are ordinarily harmless | They are present solely upon persons of unclean habits who seldom wash their faces | They inhabit the stomach and cause illness | They are found only upon domestic animals
exam: Ascertain the classification of the organisms commonly sold under the name «sea monkeys». | Brine shrimps | Primates of marine habit | Juvenile fish | Sea snails
